1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
An array is one block, and the address of element i is arithmetic. A linked list is not a block at all.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Each element is a node: it holds a value and the address of the next node. Following that address is called a link.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:23,000
So reaching the k-th node costs k link reads, not one multiplication. Random access becomes a walk.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
The list is not an object in memory. It is a head pointer plus whatever chain of nodes that pointer reaches.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
struct node { int data; struct node *next; } — a struct may hold a pointer to its own type, but not an object of its own type.

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The next field holds an address, or NULL. NULL ends the list: the empty list is head == NULL, and the last node points at nothing.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:53,000
sizeof of the node is not the sum of the members: the compiler inserts padding so the pointer starts at an aligned address.

8
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Nothing stores the length. So counting the nodes costs a walk — unless you keep a separate count, which is a design choice with its own cost.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Inserting after p needs no search and no shifting: x->next = p->next, then p->next = x. Two assignments.

10
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:15,000
GATE 1998 asked what four assignments do: q = p->next; p->next = q->next; q->next = (q->next)->next; (p->next)->next = q.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Trace with addresses, not arrows. Give each node a number, write the field values, and the swap becomes four overwrites.

12
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Deleting after p is one assignment, but save the victim first or the memory leaks. Deleting p itself is the harder problem.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Inserting at the front changes which node is first, so it changes the head pointer itself.

14
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:45,000
A parameter node *head is a copy. Assigning to it inside the function changes the copy, and the caller never sees it.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:53,000
GATE 2010: after the loop, p is the last node and q the one before it. The blank is q->next = NULL; p->next = head; head = p.

16
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
The order rule: detach the tail, then attach it to the old front, then move the head. Reverse the last two and the list is lost.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,000
Reaching the k-th element: constant time in an array, k steps in a list. That one row drives most of the table.

18
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Binary search needs the middle, and a list cannot reach the middle without walking to it. So a sorted list searches in linear time.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Given a pointer to the node, insertion is constant time in both. Deleting the node you were given is O(n) in a singly linked list and O(1) in a doubly linked one.

20
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Copying the successor into the given node gives O(1) deletion in a singly linked list, but it fails on the last node, so the worst case stays O(n).

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,000
A doubly linked node stores prev and next. The invariant: for every node b, b->next->prev == b and b->prev->next == b.

22
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Splicing x between a and b touches four pointers: x->next = b, x->prev = a, a->next = x, b->prev = x.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Deleting x from a pointer to x alone: x->prev->next = x->next; x->next->prev = x->prev. Two assignments, no walk.

24
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:00,000
GATE 2016 Set 2: N deletes cost N, N finds cost N squared over log factors, but N decrease-keys each walk forward, giving N squared overall.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,000
In a circular list the last node points at the first, not at NULL. There is no terminator, so you must supply the stopping rule.

26
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:15,000
GATE 2004: point the single pointer at the rear. The front is then p->next, so dequeue is one assignment and enqueue is one insertion.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Point it at the front instead and dequeue stays constant, but enqueue needs the rear, which is a full walk.

28
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:30,000
GATE 1997: concatenating two lists in constant time needs the last node of the first without a walk. A circular doubly linked list gives it as head->prev.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Reversal rewrites every node next to point backwards. Three cursors: prev, curr and next.

30
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:45,000
Each step: save next = curr->next; curr->next = prev; prev = curr; curr = next. The save comes first.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:53,000
n nodes, n link writes, n steps: linear time in constant extra space. That is exactly GATE 2022.

32
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:00,000
The recursive reversal is also linear time, but its recursion depth is n, so its space is linear. Only the loop is O(1) space.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Merging two sorted lists: two cursors, one output cursor, and take the smaller head each time.

34
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Comparisons: at most n1 + n2 − 1, because each comparison consumes one node and the last node is attached without one.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:23,000
A cycle is found by two cursors at different speeds: the tortoise moves one link, the hare moves two. If there is a cycle they meet.

36
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:30,000
With the cycle entering after mu links and repeating every lambda, the hare gains one link per step, so they meet within mu + lambda steps.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,000
First question: is a pointer to the node given, or must it be found? That decides constant against linear in almost every option.

38
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Second: which pointers must change — next only, or next and prev? Third: is the head itself changing?

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Fourth: is the list circular or NULL-terminated? A loop that tests for NULL on a circular list never terminates.

40
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Next in this repository: trees, binary search trees and heaps — a node with two links instead of one, and the same pointer surgery.

